  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nCurrently have a sharp upper trough extending from the Great Basin \ndown into Southern CA with upper ridging centered over the TX Gulf \nCoast. At the surface, cold front extends from Northern IA down \nthrough southern Nebraska and into far NW KS with a surface trough \njust ahead of the front.\n\nThere will be a chance early this evening for a few showers and \nstorms to develop across central KS near the surface trough as it \nsags south. This will also be in an area of enhanced mid level theta-\ne advection. This activity will quickly lift off to the northeast \nthis evening. \n\nBy Tue afternoon, cold front is expected to stretch generally \nbetween I-70 and Highway 50 with a surface low from southeast CO \ninto southwest KS. Higher resolution models are pushing the front \nfurther south compared to some of the others with confidence \nincreasing in the faster solutions. Not only would this have an\nimpact on daytime convection, but also with temps. So there's a\nchance highs on Tue could be quite a bit cooler than most of \nthe guidance, especially over central KS. Still looking for \nstorms to develop late Tue afternoon where surface convergence \nwill be maximized, which looks to be over west/central KS, \ncloser to the triple point. After sunset, storms will then \nexpand in coverage as low level jet ramps-up. Strong upper jet \nis also expected to stretch from northern NM through NW KS and \ninto the Upper Mississippi Valley with the right entrance region\nof this jet moving towards the area Tue night. With strong \nupper jet near the area, there will be enough deep layer shear \nfor some hail, especially the further north storms can develop. \nWith PW values over 200% of normal starting Tue evening, high \nrainfall rates and potential flooding will also be possible with\nany of the stronger activity.\n\nThere remains some uncertainty with cold front location by Wed \nafternoon with CAMS pushing it further south compared to other \nguidance. Current thinking is that by 18z Wed, it will stretch \nsoutheast of the KS Turnpike with best chance for surface convection \nmainly only affecting southeast KS. While some scattered showers and \nstorms will be possible during the day Wed, we are expecting an \nincrease in storms and heavy rain Wed night into Thu morning as deep \nupper low tracks from the Four Corners region into eastern CO. This \nwill allow the right entrance region of upper jet and strong \nupper diffluence to move into the area. In addition, extremely \nhigh PW airmass will remain in place along with nearly \nstationary boundary. This will lead to a high likelyhood of \nheavy rainfall with the stronger showers and storms. At this \npoint it looks like areas southeast of the KS Turnpike will have\nthe higher chances for flooding Wed night into Thu morning, due\nto them being closer to the boundary. \n\nUpper wave will quickly get sheared out on Thu as another upper \nimpulse tracks out of the Northern Plains and into the Mid \nMississippi Valley. This will push the better precip chances south \nof the area by Thu evening. Strong surface high pressure will \nquickly move-in Fri and slide east for Sat, keeping things dry and \nallowing for comfortable temps through the weekend.",
